extraction_notes: |
  - South America, printed 138 (cache p139). Printed 137 was read and is a
    full-page illustration with no text; the whole entry sits on printed 138,
    headed "Grimbor Ape-Men, Optional Player R.C.C." The stored name is the
    singular of the table-of-contents entry "Grimbor Ape-Men R.C.C.". A 110 dpi
    render of printed 138 agreed with the text layer on every number.
  - NO xp_table IS STORED. The experience tables (printed 168, cache p169)
    say "Same as the Vagabond O.C.C.", and `vagabond` in production carries no
    xp_table, so there is nothing to copy. Checked 2026-09-25.
  - S.D.C. IS `sdc_base`, NOT A POOL BONUS. The book prints "1D6X100 S.D.C.,
    standard hit points" with no "plus O.C.C." clause, and the grimbor is an
    optional player R.C.C. played as itself, the same shape as the
    first-stage-promethean''s 1d6x100. With `sdc_base` stated, no `men_of_arms`
    line is needed. Hit points are the standard P.E. + 1D6 per level.
  - BONUSES: "+6 to S.D.C. damage (in addition to P.S. bonuses)" is stored as
    `combat.damage_bonus: 6`; "+1 to parry" as `parry: 1`; "two extra attacks
    per melee" as `attacks: 2` on top of the hand to hand skill. The Horror
    Factor of 10 is the top-level `horror_factor`.
  - RELATED SKILLS ARE GRANTED EVEN THOUGH THIS IS AN R.C.C., because the book
    prints an "R.C.C. Related Skills" list and the class is played with no
    O.C.C. The book gives none at first level, "an additional two skills at
    levels 3, 6, 9, 12 and 15", so `count: 0` with that schedule. No secondary
    skills are printed and none are stored.
  - THE "AUTOMATICALLY KNOWS" SKILLS ARE STORED AT THE PRINTED FIGURE WITH NO
    PER-LEVEL GAIN (identify plants 60, land navigation 80, wilderness
    survival 80, track animal 50, carpentry 60), and Gobblely at 90 likewise.
    The book states flat percentages and no progression; a judgement call.
  - TRACKING HUMANS AND ANIMALS (+20%) is stored as `Tracking (people)` at
    25+20 = 45. The catalog has no combined row. Track & Trap Animals is the
    separate automatic "track animal 50% (males only)".
  - LANGUAGE: DRAGONESE/ELVEN is the catalog''s `Language: Dragonese`, 50+15.
  - SWIM 20% AND TRACK BY SMELL 60% ARE NATURAL ABILITIES, not skills: the
    book lists them under R.C.C. Abilities. Physical: Any still allows the
    Swimming skill as a related pick; a player taking it gets the catalog
    figure. "Terrible climbers" has no number and is prose.
  - HAND TO HAND: EXPERT, "cannot learn Martial Arts or Assassin", is
    `hand_to_hand: { costs: {} }` - no upgrade offered.
  - RELATED CATEGORIES: Communications, Mechanical, Military and Pilot Related
    are printed None and omitted. Espionage is "Wilderness survival only",
    which the class already knows at 80%, so it is omitted rather than listed
    as a redundant cross-category `only`. Pilot "ground vehicles only" is
    stored as an `only` list of every land-vehicle row in the catalog
    (Automobile, Bicycling, Combat Driving, Hover Craft (ground), Motorcycles
    & Snowmobiles, Tracked & Construction Vehicles, Truck, Military: Tanks &
    APCs) - a judgement call; hovercycles and robots are left out. Technical
    "art, language and lore only" is Art, Language: Other and every Technical
    Lore row the catalog held on 2026-09-25; a Lore row added later is not
    reached. Medical is Holistic Medicine only at +5%. Wilderness Any +10%.
  - MALES ONLY: the skill list is the Cibolan military training given to
    males, and the track-animal and track-by-smell figures are marked males
    only. The book gives no female skill list; the class models the male
    warrior and says so in its restrictions.
  - EQUIPMENT: the NE-10 rifle, First-Aid Kit and Web Belt (military) are
    existing rows; the ammo bandolier is the generic
    `bandoleer-with-pouches-and-or-belt-loops`. THREE ROWS FROM add-south-america-rcc-gear.sql: the grimbor body
    armor (120 M.D.C. plus a 35 M.D.C. force field, unusable by normal
    humanoids), the high-tech yumbuto club (energized, 3D6 M.D.), and the
    NE-10 magazine (8 spares; printed 139 gives the rifle a 20-shot magazine
    at 800 credits). None has a stat block of its own in the book beyond
    those figures.
  - Money is 1D6X100 credits.
---

## Lore

The grimbor are great apes from the Palladium World, close to Earth''s gorilla
in shape and temper but smarter, tool-using, and trained in modern weapons,
armor and equipment. Mild and compassionate by nature, they fight savagely
when they must. Their numbers in their native jungles have long been falling.

The alien intelligence Inix, taking the shape of a huge grimbor and calling
himself their god and protector, won the fealty of tribe after tribe and moved
thousands of them through dimensional gates to Cibola and the jungle around
it. Most live as semi-nomadic hunter-gatherers; every male who comes of age
owes Cibola years of military service as a scout or guerrilla fighter.

## GM Notes

Inix spends the grimbor as cannon fodder, and many tribes are left short of
men or burdened with maimed veterans, yet most still worship him. The pincer
warriors bully them; the pogtalian dragon slayers respect them, and mixed
tribes of the two races turn up in the Amazon and beyond. Cibolan scouts and
warriors are issued armor built for the grimbor frame, the Naruni NE-10
plasma cartridge rifle, whose ammunition is sold nowhere else in South
America, and an energized version of the tribal yumbuto club.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'grimbor');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'grimbor';

